71
00:02:04,342 --> 00:02:06,276
If you remember one thing from this

72
00:02:06,276 --> 00:02:07,546
section, remember that.

73
00:02:07,726 --> 00:02:09,125
This is education,

74
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:10,757
not financial advice.

75
00:02:11,106 --> 00:02:13,034
Let's start with where most people are.

76
00:02:13,214 --> 00:02:16,290
Bankrate surveyed Americans in December twenty twenty

77
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:16,581
five.

78
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:19,219
Only forty seven percent said they had

79
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:21,419
enough money or access to funds to

80
00:02:21,419 --> 00:02:23,878
cover a one thousand dollar emergency.

81
00:02:24,058 --> 00:02:25,079
Fewer than half.

82
00:02:25,259 --> 00:02:27,268
For a one thousand dollar problem.

83
00:02:27,618 --> 00:02:28,779
It gets sharper.

84
00:02:28,959 --> 00:02:31,576
In another Bankrate survey from May twenty

85
00:02:31,576 --> 00:02:34,380
twenty five, twenty four percent of Americans

86
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:36,685
said they had no emergency savings at

87
00:02:36,685 --> 00:02:36,935
all.

88
00:02:37,115 --> 00:02:39,213
Nearly one in four people is one

89
00:02:39,213 --> 00:02:40,853
flat tire away from debt.

90
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:42,653
And at the other end,

91
00:02:42,653 --> 00:02:45,346
only twenty seven percent said they had

92
00:02:45,346 --> 00:02:47,901
six months or more of expenses saved.

93
00:02:48,081 --> 00:02:50,649
That's the group this video is really

94
00:02:50,649 --> 00:02:51,065
about.

95
00:02:51,245 --> 00:02:53,347
The people who crossed the line.

96
00:02:53,697 --> 00:02:55,409
Now look at what a typical family

97
00:02:55,409 --> 00:02:56,344
keeps in the bank.

98
00:02:56,524 --> 00:02:59,382
The Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances

99
00:02:59,382 --> 00:03:01,774
found that the median American family had

100
00:03:01,774 --> 00:03:04,807
about eight thousand dollars in transaction accounts

101
00:03:04,807 --> 00:03:06,032
in twenty twenty two.

102
00:03:06,212 --> 00:03:09,347
That's checking, savings and similar accounts combined.

103
00:03:09,527 --> 00:03:11,770
Median means half of families had less

104
00:03:11,770 --> 00:03:12,360
than that.

105
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:15,006
So twenty thousand dollars is about two

106
00:03:15,006 --> 00:03:16,949
and a half times what the typical

107
00:03:16,949 --> 00:03:18,422
family keeps in the bank.

108
00:03:18,602 --> 00:03:19,658
It is not rich.

109
00:03:19,838 --> 00:03:21,092
But it puts you in a very

110
00:03:21,092 --> 00:03:23,199
different position from most of the people

111
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:23,751
around you.

112
00:03:23,931 --> 00:03:26,394
And that position changes behavior in ways

113
00:03:26,394 --> 00:03:27,332
you can measure.

114
00:03:27,683 --> 00:03:30,150
Number one is the one everybody feels

115
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:30,550
first.

116
00:03:30,901 --> 00:03:32,120
Change number one.

117
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,668
Emergencies stop being emergencies.

118
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:37,270
When your car needs a new transmission,

119
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:38,387
or a tooth cracks,

120
00:03:38,387 --> 00:03:39,940
or the water heater dies,

121
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:41,802
the problem is still annoying.

122
00:03:41,982 --> 00:03:43,736
But it's no longer a crisis.

123
00:03:43,916 --> 00:03:44,740
It's a bill.

124
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:45,566
You pay it,

125
00:03:45,566 --> 00:03:46,271
you move on,

126
00:03:46,271 --> 00:03:47,915
and you rebuild the cushion.

127
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:50,469
This matters more than it sounds,

128
00:03:50,469 --> 00:03:53,072
because unexpected expenses are normal.

129
00:03:53,252 --> 00:03:55,771
In the Federal Reserve's latest survey,

130
00:03:55,771 --> 00:03:58,225
fifty nine percent of adults said they

131
00:03:58,225 --> 00:04:00,873
had at least one major unexpected expense

132
00:04:00,873 --> 00:04:01,971
in the past year.

133
00:04:02,151 --> 00:04:04,382
So the question is not whether something

134
00:04:04,382 --> 00:04:04,996
will break.

135
00:04:05,176 --> 00:04:07,045
It's whether you'll have to borrow when

136
00:04:07,045 --> 00:04:07,428
it does.

137
00:04:07,778 --> 00:04:10,561
Without savings, a two thousand dollar repair

138
00:04:10,561 --> 00:04:11,922
goes on a credit card.

139
00:04:12,102 --> 00:04:14,394
With interest, that repair can cost you

140
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:16,568
far more than two thousand dollars by

141
00:04:16,568 --> 00:04:17,919
the time it's paid off.

142
00:04:18,099 --> 00:04:20,181
With twenty thousand in the bank,

143
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:22,515
the same repair costs exactly what it

144
00:04:22,515 --> 00:04:22,894
costs.

145
00:04:23,074 --> 00:04:23,968
Nothing more.

146
00:04:24,148 --> 00:04:27,055
That difference, repeated over a lifetime,

147
00:04:27,055 --> 00:04:27,886
is enormous.

148
00:04:28,237 --> 00:04:30,199
Here's a real way to picture it.

149
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:31,896
Imagine two neighbors,

150
00:04:31,896 --> 00:04:33,896
both earning the same salary.

151
00:04:34,076 --> 00:04:36,330
Their cars break down the same week,

152
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:39,022
and both repairs cost two thousand dollars.

153
00:04:39,202 --> 00:04:41,391
The first neighbor has savings and pays

154
00:04:41,391 --> 00:04:41,896
the shop.

155
00:04:42,076 --> 00:04:42,354
Done.

156
00:04:42,534 --> 00:04:44,245
The second puts it on a card

157
00:04:44,245 --> 00:04:46,689
at over twenty percent interest and pays

158
00:04:46,689 --> 00:04:47,422
the minimum.

159
00:04:47,602 --> 00:04:49,911
Months later he's still paying for that

160
00:04:49,911 --> 00:04:52,280
repair, and the next surprise is already

161
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:52,931
on its way.

162
00:04:53,111 --> 00:04:54,145
Same income.

163
00:04:54,325 --> 00:04:55,416
Same bad luck.

164
00:04:55,596 --> 00:04:57,291
Completely different outcome.

165
00:04:57,471 --> 00:04:58,541
Try this today:

166
00:04:58,541 --> 00:05:01,465
write down the three most likely surprise

167
00:05:01,465 --> 00:05:02,821
bills in your life,

168
00:05:02,821 --> 00:05:03,748
a car repair,

169
00:05:03,748 --> 00:05:04,818
a medical bill,

170
00:05:04,818 --> 00:05:05,674
a trip home,

171
00:05:05,674 --> 00:05:07,742
and put a real number next to

172
00:05:07,742 --> 00:05:08,384
each one.

173
00:05:08,564 --> 00:05:10,329
That's your first savings target.

174
00:05:10,679 --> 00:05:11,979
Change number two.

175
00:05:12,159 --> 00:05:14,376
You stop paying what people call the

176
00:05:14,376 --> 00:05:14,992
broke tax.

177
00:05:15,172 --> 00:05:16,743
These are the costs that only exist

178
00:05:16,743 --> 00:05:18,225
because you don't have a cushion.

179
00:05:18,405 --> 00:05:19,647
Overdraft fees.

180
00:05:19,827 --> 00:05:20,884
Late fees.

181
00:05:21,064 --> 00:05:22,887
Interest on small balances.

182
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:25,307
Short term loans with brutal rates.

183
00:05:25,658 --> 00:05:26,958
Here's the worst example.

184
00:05:27,138 --> 00:05:30,589
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau explains that

185
00:05:30,589 --> 00:05:32,570
a typical two week payday loan,

186
00:05:32,570 --> 00:05:34,679
with a fee of fifteen dollars for

187
00:05:34,679 --> 00:05:36,660
every hundred dollars borrowed,

188
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:39,089
works out to an annual percentage rate

189
00:05:39,089 --> 00:05:41,070
of almost four hundred percent.

190
00:05:41,250 --> 00:05:42,248
Four hundred.

191
00:05:42,428 --> 00:05:44,583
People don't take those loans because they

192
00:05:44,583 --> 00:05:45,250
are careless.

193
00:05:45,430 --> 00:05:47,343
They take them because they have no

194
00:05:47,343 --> 00:05:48,053
other option.

195
00:05:48,404 --> 00:05:50,213
Credit cards are less extreme,

196
00:05:50,213 --> 00:05:51,178
but they add up.

197
00:05:51,358 --> 00:05:54,278
The Federal Reserve publishes average credit card

198
00:05:54,278 --> 00:05:56,364
rates, and in recent years the rate

199
00:05:56,364 --> 00:05:58,629
on accounts that pay interest has been

200
00:05:58,629 --> 00:05:59,880
above twenty percent.

201
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,466
When you have twenty thousand dollars in

202
00:06:02,466 --> 00:06:04,330
cash, you can pay every card in

203
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:05,412
full, every month.

204
00:06:05,592 --> 00:06:07,604
That one habit alone can save you

205
00:06:07,604 --> 00:06:09,737
thousands of dollars over a decade.

206
00:06:10,087 --> 00:06:12,107
And here's the part people miss.

207
00:06:12,287 --> 00:06:14,400
The broke tax doesn't just cost money.

208
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:16,658
It costs time and attention.

209
00:06:16,838 --> 00:06:17,888
Once it's gone,

210
00:06:17,888 --> 00:06:19,148
you get both back.

211
00:06:19,499 --> 00:06:21,766
How much does the broke tax really

212
00:06:21,766 --> 00:06:22,099
cost?

213
00:06:22,279 --> 00:06:23,463
Add it up for a year.

214
00:06:23,643 --> 00:06:25,096
A few overdraft fees,

215
00:06:25,096 --> 00:06:26,342
a late fee or two,

216
00:06:26,342 --> 00:06:28,971
interest on a balance you couldn't pay

217
00:06:28,971 --> 00:06:31,115
off, maybe a rush fee because a

218
00:06:31,115 --> 00:06:32,084
bill was late.

219
00:06:32,264 --> 00:06:35,161
For many households that easily reaches hundreds

220
00:06:35,161 --> 00:06:36,247
of dollars a year,

221
00:06:36,247 --> 00:06:38,359
money that buys absolutely nothing.

222
00:06:38,539 --> 00:06:40,754
Try this: open your last three bank

223
00:06:40,754 --> 00:06:43,476
and card statements and highlight every fee

224
00:06:43,476 --> 00:06:45,122
and every interest charge.

225
00:06:45,302 --> 00:06:47,427
Most people are surprised by the total.

226
00:06:47,607 --> 00:06:49,878
That total is what your cushion will

227
00:06:49,878 --> 00:06:51,644
save you, every single year,

228
00:06:51,644 --> 00:06:52,401
from now on.

229
00:06:52,752 --> 00:06:54,017
Change number three.

230
00:06:54,197 --> 00:06:55,846
You get the power to say no.

231
00:06:56,026 --> 00:06:57,558
No to a toxic boss.

232
00:06:57,738 --> 00:06:59,566
No to a job that's slowly wrecking

233
00:06:59,566 --> 00:07:00,211
your health.

234
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:02,400
No to a deal that feels wrong.

235
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:04,611
Some people call this walk away money.

236
00:07:04,962 --> 00:07:06,738
Think about it in months instead of

237
00:07:06,738 --> 00:07:07,144
dollars.

238
00:07:07,324 --> 00:07:09,642
If your basic monthly costs are around

239
00:07:09,642 --> 00:07:10,984
four thousand dollars,

240
00:07:10,984 --> 00:07:13,669
twenty thousand dollars is about five months

241
00:07:13,669 --> 00:07:14,279
of runway.

242
00:07:14,459 --> 00:07:16,209
That is enough time to look for

243
00:07:16,209 --> 00:07:17,733
a better job without panic.

244
00:07:17,913 --> 00:07:20,156
And people who search without panic tend

245
00:07:20,156 --> 00:07:21,278
to negotiate better,

246
00:07:21,278 --> 00:07:23,242
because they can wait for the right

247
00:07:23,242 --> 00:07:23,578
offer.

248
00:07:23,929 --> 00:07:25,508
Here's that math on screen.

249
00:07:25,688 --> 00:07:28,623
Four thousand dollars a month in essential

250
00:07:28,623 --> 00:07:29,043
costs.

251
00:07:29,223 --> 00:07:31,173
Twenty thousand dollars saved.

252
00:07:31,353 --> 00:07:33,705
Five months where your decisions belong to

253
00:07:33,705 --> 00:07:35,440
you, not to your next paycheck.

254
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:38,144
Walk away money also changes how you're

255
00:07:38,144 --> 00:07:39,110
treated at work,

256
00:07:39,110 --> 00:07:40,619
even if you never use it.

257
00:07:40,799 --> 00:07:42,954
People who don't need the job can

258
00:07:42,954 --> 00:07:44,325
speak up in meetings,

259
00:07:44,325 --> 00:07:45,827
push back on bad ideas,

260
00:07:45,827 --> 00:07:47,916
and turn down extra unpaid work.

261
00:07:48,096 --> 00:07:49,830
They are often the ones who get

262
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:52,571
noticed, because they act like professionals with

263
00:07:52,571 --> 00:07:53,019
options.

264
00:07:53,199 --> 00:07:54,975
The cushion doesn't make you braver.

265
00:07:55,155 --> 00:07:56,525
It makes bravery cheaper.

266
00:07:56,705 --> 00:07:59,155
Try this: calculate your own runway.

267
00:07:59,335 --> 00:08:00,351
Add up rent,

268
00:08:00,351 --> 00:08:02,637
food, utilities, insurance,

269
00:08:02,637 --> 00:08:05,686
transport and minimum debt payments.

270
00:08:05,866 --> 00:08:07,793
Divide your savings by that number.

271
00:08:07,973 --> 00:08:09,852
That's how many months of freedom you

272
00:08:09,852 --> 00:08:10,411
have today.

273
00:08:10,761 --> 00:08:12,049
Change number four.

274
00:08:12,229 --> 00:08:14,130
Your credit gets stronger,

275
00:08:14,130 --> 00:08:15,666
almost automatically.

276
00:08:15,846 --> 00:08:17,894
Not because savings show up on your

277
00:08:17,894 --> 00:08:18,714
credit report.

278
00:08:18,894 --> 00:08:19,613
They don't.

279
00:08:19,793 --> 00:08:21,860
But because cash in the bank changes

280
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:22,951
how you use credit.

281
00:08:23,302 --> 00:08:25,062
Two things matter a lot in a

282
00:08:25,062 --> 00:08:25,879
credit score.

283
00:08:26,059 --> 00:08:26,868
Paying on time,

284
00:08:26,868 --> 00:08:28,864
and how much of your available credit

285
00:08:28,864 --> 00:08:29,565
you're using.

286
00:08:29,745 --> 00:08:31,167
When you have a cushion,

287
00:08:31,167 --> 00:08:33,419
you never miss a payment because money

288
00:08:33,419 --> 00:08:35,493
is tight, and you stop carrying big

289
00:08:35,493 --> 00:08:36,026
balances.

290
00:08:36,206 --> 00:08:38,099
Both of those push your score up

291
00:08:38,099 --> 00:08:38,691
over time.

292
00:08:39,041 --> 00:08:41,177
A better score is not a trophy.

293
00:08:41,357 --> 00:08:43,287
It means lower rates on a car

294
00:08:43,287 --> 00:08:44,419
loan, a mortgage,

295
00:08:44,419 --> 00:08:47,348
and sometimes even insurance in some states.

296
00:08:47,528 --> 00:08:48,537
Over a lifetime,

297
00:08:48,537 --> 00:08:50,934
a few percentage points of interest on

298
00:08:50,934 --> 00:08:52,701
big loans can add up to tens

299
00:08:52,701 --> 00:08:54,215
of thousands of dollars.

300
00:08:54,395 --> 00:08:56,380
So the cushion pays you twice.

301
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:58,077
Once when you avoid debt,

302
00:08:58,077 --> 00:09:00,202
and again when you borrow on better

303
00:09:00,202 --> 00:09:00,566
terms.

304
00:09:00,916 --> 00:09:02,890
Here's an example of how it works

305
00:09:02,890 --> 00:09:03,667
in real life.

306
00:09:03,847 --> 00:09:06,710
Someone carries a three thousand dollar balance

307
00:09:06,710 --> 00:09:08,477
on a card with a ten thousand

308
00:09:08,477 --> 00:09:09,269
dollar limit.

309
00:09:09,449 --> 00:09:11,935
They're using thirty percent of their available

310
00:09:11,935 --> 00:09:12,305
credit.

311
00:09:12,485 --> 00:09:13,944
Once they have savings,

312
00:09:13,944 --> 00:09:15,656
they pay it off and keep it

313
00:09:15,656 --> 00:09:17,685
at zero, using the card only for

314
00:09:17,685 --> 00:09:19,207
things they pay in full.

315
00:09:19,387 --> 00:09:21,776
Their utilization drops close to zero,

316
00:09:21,776 --> 00:09:24,354
and over the following months their score

317
00:09:24,354 --> 00:09:25,297
usually climbs.

318
00:09:25,477 --> 00:09:27,980
Try this: set every card to autopay

319
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,912
the full statement balance.

320
00:09:30,092 --> 00:09:31,680
With a cushion behind you,

321
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:34,122
that's safe, and it's the single easiest

322
00:09:34,122 --> 00:09:36,199
way to never miss a payment again.

323
00:09:36,549 --> 00:09:38,267
Now here's where it gets fun.

324
00:09:38,447 --> 00:09:39,411
Number five.

325
00:09:39,761 --> 00:09:41,073
Change number five.

326
00:09:41,253 --> 00:09:42,948
Your money starts earning money.

327
00:09:43,128 --> 00:09:45,194
Even sitting in a savings account,

328
00:09:45,194 --> 00:09:47,806
twenty thousand dollars produces something.

329
00:09:47,986 --> 00:09:49,890
If an account pays four percent a

330
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:52,314
year, that's about eight hundred dollars a

331
00:09:52,314 --> 00:09:53,699
year, for doing nothing.

332
00:09:54,049 --> 00:09:56,300
Eight hundred dollars won't change your life

333
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:56,812
by itself.

334
00:09:56,992 --> 00:09:58,873
But it changes how you see money.

335
00:09:59,053 --> 00:10:00,010
For the first time,

336
00:10:00,010 --> 00:10:01,873
money is not only something you trade

337
00:10:01,873 --> 00:10:02,628
your hours for.

338
00:10:02,808 --> 00:10:04,719
It is something that grows while you

339
00:10:04,719 --> 00:10:05,038
sleep.

340
00:10:05,218 --> 00:10:06,582
Rates change over time,

341
00:10:06,582 --> 00:10:08,896
so always check what your bank actually

342
00:10:08,896 --> 00:10:09,548
pays today.

343
00:10:09,898 --> 00:10:11,141
And when you keep going,

344
00:10:11,141 --> 00:10:12,591
time does the heavy lifting.

345
00:10:12,771 --> 00:10:14,582
One hundred dollars a month,

346
00:10:14,582 --> 00:10:16,263
invested for twenty years,

347
00:10:16,263 --> 00:10:18,914
at a seven percent average yearly return,

348
00:10:18,914 --> 00:10:21,630
grows to about fifty two thousand dollars.

349
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:23,725
You put in twenty four thousand.

350
00:10:23,905 --> 00:10:25,055
The rest is growth.

351
00:10:25,235 --> 00:10:27,609
Again, that's an illustration,

352
00:10:27,609 --> 00:10:28,718
not a promise.

353
00:10:29,068 --> 00:10:31,285
Something else happens at this level.

354
00:10:31,465 --> 00:10:33,961
You start comparing where your money sits.

355
00:10:34,141 --> 00:10:36,265
A big bank checking account might pay

356
00:10:36,265 --> 00:10:37,241
close to nothing.

357
00:10:37,421 --> 00:10:40,077
An online high yield savings account might

358
00:10:40,077 --> 00:10:40,962
pay much more.

359
00:10:41,142 --> 00:10:42,739
On twenty thousand dollars,

360
00:10:42,739 --> 00:10:45,104
the difference between almost zero and a

361
00:10:45,104 --> 00:10:47,232
few percent is hundreds of dollars a

362
00:10:47,232 --> 00:10:49,242
year, just for moving the money to

363
00:10:49,242 --> 00:10:50,720
a better insured account.

364
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,201
Try this: look up what your current

365
00:10:53,201 --> 00:10:54,581
savings account pays.

366
00:10:54,761 --> 00:10:56,041
If it's close to zero,

367
00:10:56,041 --> 00:10:57,962
you are leaving free money on the

368
00:10:57,962 --> 00:10:59,010
table every month.

369
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:00,799
Change number six.

370
00:11:00,979 --> 00:11:02,500
You make better decisions.

371
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:04,698
This one is backed by research on

372
00:11:04,698 --> 00:11:06,593
what scarcity does to the mind.

373
00:11:06,943 --> 00:11:11,161
Economist Sendhil Mullainathan and psychologist Eldar Shafir

374
00:11:11,161 --> 00:11:13,200
wrote a book called Scarcity.

375
00:11:13,380 --> 00:11:14,762
Their argument is simple.

376
00:11:14,942 --> 00:11:16,502
When you are short on money,

377
00:11:16,502 --> 00:11:18,843
your mind gets pulled toward the shortage.

378
00:11:19,023 --> 00:11:20,655
Rent, the next bill,

379
00:11:20,655 --> 00:11:21,798
the overdraft.

380
00:11:21,978 --> 00:11:23,700
That pull uses up attention,

381
00:11:23,700 --> 00:11:25,915
and it leaves less room for planning

382
00:11:25,915 --> 00:11:27,330
and long term thinking.

383
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:30,156
It's not that people without money are

384
00:11:30,156 --> 00:11:31,395
worse at decisions.

385
00:11:31,575 --> 00:11:32,737
It's that anyone,

386
00:11:32,737 --> 00:11:35,063
put under constant money pressure,

387
00:11:35,063 --> 00:11:36,567
starts deciding worse.

388
00:11:36,747 --> 00:11:39,162
A cushion removes some of that pressure.

389
00:11:39,342 --> 00:11:40,976
And with the pressure gone,

390
00:11:40,976 --> 00:11:43,034
people tend to plan further ahead,

391
00:11:43,034 --> 00:11:45,394
compare prices, and avoid the quick fix

392
00:11:45,394 --> 00:11:46,726
that costs more later.

393
00:11:47,076 --> 00:11:49,131
In practice, it looks like this.

394
00:11:49,311 --> 00:11:52,035
Without savings, a fridge breaks and you

395
00:11:52,035 --> 00:11:53,942
buy the first one that fits,

396
00:11:53,942 --> 00:11:55,031
on a store card,

397
00:11:55,031 --> 00:11:55,917
the same day.

398
00:11:56,097 --> 00:11:58,265
With savings, you wait two days,

399
00:11:58,265 --> 00:11:59,688
compare three models,

400
00:11:59,688 --> 00:12:01,246
check the energy costs,

401
00:12:01,246 --> 00:12:03,956
buy the one that's actually cheaper over

402
00:12:03,956 --> 00:12:05,582
ten years, and pay cash.

403
00:12:05,762 --> 00:12:06,633
Same problem.

404
00:12:06,813 --> 00:12:07,765
Better decision.

405
00:12:07,945 --> 00:12:09,466
Lower total cost.

406
00:12:09,646 --> 00:12:12,157
Try this: for any purchase over two

407
00:12:12,157 --> 00:12:15,314
hundred dollars, give yourself a forty eight

408
00:12:15,314 --> 00:12:16,031
hour rule.

409
00:12:16,211 --> 00:12:17,953
If you still want it after two

410
00:12:17,953 --> 00:12:18,707
days, buy it.

411
00:12:18,887 --> 00:12:20,035
Most of the time,

412
00:12:20,035 --> 00:12:20,710
you won't.

413
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:22,395
Change number seven.

414
00:12:22,575 --> 00:12:24,711
You stop financing small things.

415
00:12:24,891 --> 00:12:26,702
Phones on monthly plans.

416
00:12:26,882 --> 00:12:28,635
Furniture in four payments.

417
00:12:28,815 --> 00:12:31,126
Sneakers on buy now pay later.

418
00:12:31,476 --> 00:12:34,784
Splitting a purchase into payments feels harmless.

419
00:12:34,964 --> 00:12:36,897
But every small payment is a claim

420
00:12:36,897 --> 00:12:38,262
on your future paycheck.

421
00:12:38,442 --> 00:12:40,024
Stack a few of them and a

422
00:12:40,024 --> 00:12:42,112
big part of your income is spoken

423
00:12:42,112 --> 00:12:43,504
for before it arrives.

424
00:12:43,684 --> 00:12:45,007
With cash in the bank,

425
00:12:45,007 --> 00:12:45,788
you just pay,

426
00:12:45,788 --> 00:12:47,712
or you decide you don't need it.

427
00:12:47,892 --> 00:12:49,944
Both options are better than a slow

428
00:12:49,944 --> 00:12:51,410
drip of monthly payments.

429
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:52,990
Quick rule I like.

430
00:12:53,170 --> 00:12:54,815
If you can't buy it twice in

431
00:12:54,815 --> 00:12:57,106
cash, you probably can't afford it yet.

432
00:12:57,286 --> 00:12:59,639
Now, number eight is one most people

433
00:12:59,639 --> 00:13:00,816
never think about.

434
00:13:01,166 --> 00:13:03,184
The trick is that each small payment

435
00:13:03,184 --> 00:13:04,753
looks affordable on its own.

436
00:13:04,933 --> 00:13:06,257
Thirty dollars here,

437
00:13:06,257 --> 00:13:07,580
forty dollars there.

438
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:09,867
But add up every installment plan,

439
00:13:09,867 --> 00:13:12,594
every financing deal and every subscription,

440
00:13:12,594 --> 00:13:15,321
and many people discover hundreds of dollars

441
00:13:15,321 --> 00:13:16,932
a month already committed.

442
00:13:17,112 --> 00:13:18,893
That's money that can't go to the

443
00:13:18,893 --> 00:13:20,189
cushion or to investing.

444
00:13:20,369 --> 00:13:23,257
Try this: list every recurring payment you

445
00:13:23,257 --> 00:13:24,839
have, every single one,

446
00:13:24,839 --> 00:13:25,733
and total it.

447
00:13:25,913 --> 00:13:28,571
Then cancel or pay off the smallest

448
00:13:28,571 --> 00:13:29,407
ones first.

449
00:13:29,587 --> 00:13:31,744
Each one you clear frees up cash

450
00:13:31,744 --> 00:13:32,350
for good.

451
00:13:32,701 --> 00:13:33,943
Change number eight.

452
00:13:34,123 --> 00:13:35,899
You can pay less for insurance.

453
00:13:36,079 --> 00:13:37,470
When you have savings,

454
00:13:37,470 --> 00:13:39,683
you can choose a higher deductible,

455
00:13:39,683 --> 00:13:42,149
and a higher deductible usually means a

456
00:13:42,149 --> 00:13:43,034
lower premium.

457
00:13:43,384 --> 00:13:45,363
The deductible is the part you pay

458
00:13:45,363 --> 00:13:47,401
yourself before insurance kicks in.

459
00:13:47,581 --> 00:13:50,252
If a five hundred dollar deductible feels

460
00:13:50,252 --> 00:13:52,662
scary, you pay a higher premium every

461
00:13:52,662 --> 00:13:54,291
single month to avoid it.

462
00:13:54,471 --> 00:13:56,780
With twenty thousand dollars saved,

463
00:13:56,780 --> 00:13:59,353
a one thousand dollar deductible is not

464
00:13:59,353 --> 00:14:00,276
scary anymore.

465
00:14:00,456 --> 00:14:02,388
You can take on that small risk

466
00:14:02,388 --> 00:14:04,195
and keep the monthly savings.

467
00:14:04,545 --> 00:14:06,746
Done carefully, this is one of the

468
00:14:06,746 --> 00:14:09,142
quiet ways people with money pay less

469
00:14:09,142 --> 00:14:10,373
for the same thing.

470
00:14:10,553 --> 00:14:12,693
They can afford to self insure the

471
00:14:12,693 --> 00:14:15,147
small stuff, so they only buy insurance

472
00:14:15,147 --> 00:14:16,532
for the big disasters.

473
00:14:16,712 --> 00:14:18,906
That's exactly what insurance is for.

474
00:14:19,257 --> 00:14:20,766
Here's how the math can work.

475
00:14:20,946 --> 00:14:23,619
Say raising a deductible from five hundred

476
00:14:23,619 --> 00:14:26,165
to one thousand dollars lowers a premium

477
00:14:26,165 --> 00:14:28,074
by a certain amount each year.

478
00:14:28,254 --> 00:14:30,312
If you go several years without a

479
00:14:30,312 --> 00:14:32,620
claim, you keep all of those savings.

480
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:34,100
If you do have a claim,

481
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:36,249
you pay five hundred dollars more that

482
00:14:36,249 --> 00:14:38,454
year, which your cushion covers easily.

483
00:14:38,634 --> 00:14:39,680
Over many years,

484
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:42,361
people with savings often come out ahead.

485
00:14:42,541 --> 00:14:44,606
Try this: ask your insurer for a

486
00:14:44,606 --> 00:14:46,607
quote with a higher deductible,

487
00:14:46,607 --> 00:14:47,382
and compare.

488
00:14:47,562 --> 00:14:49,548
It's a five minute phone call that

489
00:14:49,548 --> 00:14:51,300
can pay for itself every year.

490
00:14:51,651 --> 00:14:52,916
Change number nine.

491
00:14:53,096 --> 00:14:54,234
You can negotiate.

492
00:14:54,414 --> 00:14:56,331
Cash on hand gives you options,

493
00:14:56,331 --> 00:14:58,187
and options give you leverage.

494
00:14:58,537 --> 00:15:00,600
Some sellers will lower a price for

495
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:01,544
payment in full.

496
00:15:01,724 --> 00:15:04,231
Some service providers offer a discount if

497
00:15:04,231 --> 00:15:06,082
you pay a year up front instead

498
00:15:06,082 --> 00:15:06,739
of monthly.

499
00:15:06,919 --> 00:15:08,790
A used car bought with cash,

500
00:15:08,790 --> 00:15:10,060
at the right price,

501
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:11,930
can cost far less than a new

502
00:15:11,930 --> 00:15:13,467
one bought with a loan.

503
00:15:13,647 --> 00:15:15,626
None of these deals are available to

504
00:15:15,626 --> 00:15:17,440
someone who can only pay a little

505
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:17,989
at a time.

506
00:15:18,340 --> 00:15:21,027
And maybe the biggest negotiation is your

507
00:15:21,027 --> 00:15:21,486
salary.

508
00:15:21,666 --> 00:15:22,995
When you're not desperate,

509
00:15:22,995 --> 00:15:24,068
you can ask for more,

510
00:15:24,068 --> 00:15:25,499
and you can hold out for the

511
00:15:25,499 --> 00:15:26,113
right offer.

512
00:15:26,293 --> 00:15:27,512
That's not arrogance.

513
00:15:27,692 --> 00:15:29,520
It's the calm that comes from not

514
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:31,569
needing the next paycheck to survive.

515
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:34,542
Negotiating with cash is not about being

516
00:15:34,542 --> 00:15:35,263
aggressive.

517
00:15:35,443 --> 00:15:36,918
It's about being ready.

518
00:15:37,098 --> 00:15:38,208
When you can say,

519
00:15:38,208 --> 00:15:39,252
I can pay today,

520
00:15:39,252 --> 00:15:41,342
in full, what's your best price,

521
00:15:41,342 --> 00:15:43,170
you change the conversation.

522
00:15:43,350 --> 00:15:45,473
Many sellers would rather take a little

523
00:15:45,473 --> 00:15:47,378
less now than wait for maybe later.

524
00:15:47,558 --> 00:15:50,373
Try this: next time you buy something

525
00:15:50,373 --> 00:15:51,590
big, a used car,

526
00:15:51,590 --> 00:15:53,720
furniture, a contractor job,

527
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:55,546
ask that exact question.

528
00:15:55,726 --> 00:15:57,560
The worst answer you'll get is no.

529
00:15:57,911 --> 00:15:59,106
Change number ten.

530
00:15:59,286 --> 00:16:00,993
Money fights get quieter.

531
00:16:01,173 --> 00:16:02,973
Money is one of the most common

532
00:16:02,973 --> 00:16:05,004
sources of stress in relationships,

533
00:16:05,004 --> 00:16:06,630
and a cushion takes a lot of

534
00:16:06,630 --> 00:16:08,313
that tension out of the room.

535
00:16:08,664 --> 00:16:11,330
When every surprise bill is a crisis,

536
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:14,213
every surprise bill becomes an argument.

537
00:16:14,393 --> 00:16:15,310
Who spent what.

538
00:16:15,490 --> 00:16:16,953
Why there's nothing left.

539
00:16:17,133 --> 00:16:20,170
With savings, a surprise bill is just

540
00:16:20,170 --> 00:16:20,744
a bill.

541
00:16:20,924 --> 00:16:23,085
You can talk about goals instead of

542
00:16:23,085 --> 00:16:23,455
blame.

543
00:16:23,635 --> 00:16:25,400
Plans instead of panic.

544
00:16:25,750 --> 00:16:27,415
We're past the halfway point,

545
00:16:27,415 --> 00:16:29,424
and the next five changes are where

546
00:16:29,424 --> 00:16:30,974
it gets really interesting.

547
00:16:31,154 --> 00:16:32,826
Especially number fifteen.

548
00:16:33,006 --> 00:16:33,958
Stay with me.

549
00:16:34,308 --> 00:16:36,932
A cushion also lets couples plan together.

550
00:16:37,112 --> 00:16:39,613
Instead of arguing about the last surprise,

551
00:16:39,613 --> 00:16:41,299
you can sit down once a month

552
00:16:41,299 --> 00:16:42,986
and talk about the next goal.

553
00:16:43,166 --> 00:16:45,291
A vacation paid in cash.

554
00:16:45,471 --> 00:16:46,516
A down payment.

555
00:16:46,696 --> 00:16:47,868
Paying off the car.

556
00:16:48,048 --> 00:16:50,384
Shared goals turn money from a fight

557
00:16:50,384 --> 00:16:51,357
into a project.

558
00:16:51,537 --> 00:16:53,583
Try this: pick one evening a month

559
00:16:53,583 --> 00:16:55,148
for a short money meeting.

560
00:16:55,328 --> 00:16:56,466
Fifteen minutes.

561
00:16:56,646 --> 00:16:57,993
One number to check,

562
00:16:57,993 --> 00:17:00,217
the cushion, and one goal to move

563
00:17:00,217 --> 00:17:00,756
forward.

564
00:17:01,106 --> 00:17:02,638
Change number eleven.

565
00:17:02,818 --> 00:17:04,606
You start seeing opportunities.

566
00:17:04,786 --> 00:17:06,713
A course that would raise your income.

567
00:17:06,893 --> 00:17:08,658
A small side business.

568
00:17:08,838 --> 00:17:10,621
A chance to move to a city

569
00:17:10,621 --> 00:17:11,787
with better jobs.

570
00:17:12,137 --> 00:17:13,687
When you have nothing saved,

571
00:17:13,687 --> 00:17:15,847
every opportunity looks like a risk you

572
00:17:15,847 --> 00:17:16,456
can't take.

573
00:17:16,636 --> 00:17:18,367
With twenty thousand dollars,

574
00:17:18,367 --> 00:17:19,979
you can afford a smart bet.

575
00:17:20,159 --> 00:17:21,251
Not your whole cushion.

576
00:17:21,431 --> 00:17:22,655
A small part of it,

577
00:17:22,655 --> 00:17:24,910
on something that could pay off for

578
00:17:24,910 --> 00:17:25,297
years.

579
00:17:25,477 --> 00:17:28,437
That's how many small businesses actually start.

580
00:17:28,617 --> 00:17:29,979
Not with a big loan,

581
00:17:29,979 --> 00:17:31,137
but with savings.

582
00:17:31,487 --> 00:17:33,311
The rule many people use is to

583
00:17:33,311 --> 00:17:35,074
never risk the whole cushion.

584
00:17:35,254 --> 00:17:38,246
Keep the core untouched for emergencies,

585
00:17:38,246 --> 00:17:40,266
and only use a small slice,

586
00:17:40,266 --> 00:17:42,360
maybe ten or twenty percent,

587
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:45,053
for an opportunity with real upside.

588
00:17:45,233 --> 00:17:46,313
A certification.

589
00:17:46,493 --> 00:17:48,176
Tools for a side business.

590
00:17:48,356 --> 00:17:49,842
A move for a better job.

591
00:17:50,022 --> 00:17:50,891
If it works,

592
00:17:50,891 --> 00:17:52,193
your income grows.

593
00:17:52,373 --> 00:17:53,272
If it doesn't,

594
00:17:53,272 --> 00:17:55,520
your emergency fund is still there.

595
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:58,303
Try this: write down one opportunity you've

596
00:17:58,303 --> 00:18:00,362
been putting off because of money,

597
00:18:00,362 --> 00:18:02,178
and what it would really cost.

598
00:18:02,528 --> 00:18:03,979
Change number twelve.

599
00:18:04,159 --> 00:18:07,304
You start thinking in percentages instead of

600
00:18:07,304 --> 00:18:07,804
prices.

601
00:18:07,984 --> 00:18:09,192
Not "is this cheap?"

602
00:18:09,372 --> 00:18:11,239
but "what share of my money is

603
00:18:11,239 --> 00:18:11,613
this?"

604
00:18:11,963 --> 00:18:14,046
A two hundred dollar purchase is one

605
00:18:14,046 --> 00:18:15,608
percent of twenty thousand.

606
00:18:15,788 --> 00:18:18,438
That framing helps you spend without guilt

607
00:18:18,438 --> 00:18:19,448
on what matters,

608
00:18:19,448 --> 00:18:21,593
and say no faster to what doesn't.

609
00:18:21,773 --> 00:18:24,602
It's also the mindset that makes investing

610
00:18:24,602 --> 00:18:27,903
click, because investing is all about percentages

611
00:18:27,903 --> 00:18:28,577
over time.

612
00:18:28,927 --> 00:18:32,096
Percentages also protect you from small leaks.

613
00:18:32,276 --> 00:18:33,902
A coffee subscription,

614
00:18:33,902 --> 00:18:35,379
a streaming service,

615
00:18:35,379 --> 00:18:37,152
an app you forgot about.

616
00:18:37,332 --> 00:18:39,585
Each one is a tiny percentage.

617
00:18:39,765 --> 00:18:41,215
But a dozen of them can add

618
00:18:41,215 --> 00:18:42,934
up to a meaningful share of your

619
00:18:42,934 --> 00:18:43,793
monthly savings.

620
00:18:43,973 --> 00:18:45,759
When you think in percentages,

621
00:18:45,759 --> 00:18:46,830
you see the total,

622
00:18:46,830 --> 00:18:48,199
not just the price tag.

623
00:18:48,379 --> 00:18:51,166
Try this: take your monthly savings goal

624
00:18:51,166 --> 00:18:53,325
and ask what percent of it each

625
00:18:53,325 --> 00:18:54,927
recurring expense eats.

626
00:18:55,107 --> 00:18:57,200
It's a sharper way to decide what

627
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:57,580
stays.

628
00:18:57,931 --> 00:18:59,556
Change number thirteen.

629
00:18:59,736 --> 00:19:00,827
You become a target.

630
00:19:01,007 --> 00:19:02,316
As your balance grows,

631
00:19:02,316 --> 00:19:04,219
so does the number of people who

632
00:19:04,219 --> 00:19:05,350
want a piece of it.

633
00:19:05,700 --> 00:19:08,814
The Federal Reserve's latest survey found that

634
00:19:08,814 --> 00:19:12,335
twenty percent of adults experienced financial fraud

635
00:19:12,335 --> 00:19:14,163
or a scam in the past year.

636
00:19:14,343 --> 00:19:16,111
Fake investment offers,

637
00:19:16,111 --> 00:19:17,340
fake bank calls,

638
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:20,183
fake emergencies from fake relatives.

639
00:19:20,363 --> 00:19:23,045
A real cushion deserves real protection.

640
00:19:23,225 --> 00:19:24,706
Turn on account alerts,

641
00:19:24,706 --> 00:19:27,217
never move money because a caller tells

642
00:19:27,217 --> 00:19:29,599
you to, and be suspicious of anything

643
00:19:29,599 --> 00:19:31,724
that promises guaranteed returns.

644
00:19:32,074 --> 00:19:34,639
Scammers know that people with savings feel

645
00:19:34,639 --> 00:19:37,205
safe, and that's exactly what they exploit.

646
00:19:37,385 --> 00:19:39,475
A common pattern is urgency.

647
00:19:39,655 --> 00:19:41,538
Your account is compromised,

648
00:19:41,538 --> 00:19:42,883
move your money now.

649
00:19:43,063 --> 00:19:44,927
A real bank will never ask you

650
00:19:44,927 --> 00:19:46,790
to move money to a new account

651
00:19:46,790 --> 00:19:47,660
to protect it.

652
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:50,130
Try this: agree on a simple rule

653
00:19:50,130 --> 00:19:51,346
with your family.

654
00:19:51,526 --> 00:19:53,280
Any request to move money,

655
00:19:53,280 --> 00:19:55,979
from anyone, waits twenty four hours and

656
00:19:55,979 --> 00:19:58,475
gets verified by calling a number you

657
00:19:58,475 --> 00:19:59,352
already know.

658
00:19:59,532 --> 00:20:01,946
That one rule stops most scams cold.

659
00:20:02,297 --> 00:20:03,992
Change number fourteen.

660
00:20:04,172 --> 00:20:05,958
You have to decide where the money

661
00:20:05,958 --> 00:20:06,273
lives.

662
00:20:06,453 --> 00:20:07,617
And this is where a lot of

663
00:20:07,617 --> 00:20:08,601
people make a mistake.

664
00:20:08,951 --> 00:20:11,601
Emergency money should be safe and easy

665
00:20:11,601 --> 00:20:12,213
to reach.

666
00:20:12,393 --> 00:20:14,776
The FDIC insures deposits up to two

667
00:20:14,776 --> 00:20:17,840
hundred fifty thousand dollars per depositor,

668
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:18,998
per insured bank,

669
00:20:18,998 --> 00:20:21,449
for each account ownership category.

670
00:20:21,629 --> 00:20:24,083
So a savings account at an insured

671
00:20:24,083 --> 00:20:26,536
bank is protected even if the bank

672
00:20:26,536 --> 00:20:26,969
fails.

673
00:20:27,149 --> 00:20:28,911
Keep it in a separate account from

674
00:20:28,911 --> 00:20:30,777
your checking, so you don't spend it

675
00:20:30,777 --> 00:20:31,399
by accident.

676
00:20:31,749 --> 00:20:33,188
What about the stock market?

677
00:20:33,368 --> 00:20:36,155
Long term investing is how wealth grows,

678
00:20:36,155 --> 00:20:38,105
but stocks can drop a lot in

679
00:20:38,105 --> 00:20:39,011
a short time.

680
00:20:39,191 --> 00:20:41,046
Money you might need next month should

681
00:20:41,046 --> 00:20:42,658
not be in something that could be

682
00:20:42,658 --> 00:20:44,171
down twenty percent next month.

683
00:20:44,351 --> 00:20:46,546
So the cushion stays in cash.

684
00:20:46,726 --> 00:20:48,709
Money beyond the cushion is what you

685
00:20:48,709 --> 00:20:49,094
invest.

686
00:20:49,444 --> 00:20:51,896
A simple setup many people use has

687
00:20:51,896 --> 00:20:52,834
three layers.

688
00:20:53,014 --> 00:20:54,686
Checking for monthly bills.

689
00:20:54,866 --> 00:20:57,638
A separate high yield savings account for

690
00:20:57,638 --> 00:21:00,004
the cushion, three to six months of

691
00:21:00,004 --> 00:21:00,613
expenses.

692
00:21:00,793 --> 00:21:02,276
And investment accounts,

693
00:21:02,276 --> 00:21:03,884
like a retirement account,

694
00:21:03,884 --> 00:21:05,553
for everything beyond that.

695
00:21:05,733 --> 00:21:07,223
Each layer has a job,

696
00:21:07,223 --> 00:21:09,494
and money flows down from one to

697
00:21:09,494 --> 00:21:10,133
the next.

698
00:21:10,313 --> 00:21:13,039
Try this: open a separate savings account

699
00:21:13,039 --> 00:21:14,435
just for the cushion,

700
00:21:14,435 --> 00:21:15,699
and give it a name,

701
00:21:15,699 --> 00:21:16,896
like freedom fund.

702
00:21:17,076 --> 00:21:19,504
People who name their savings accounts tend

703
00:21:19,504 --> 00:21:20,408
to protect them.

704
00:21:20,758 --> 00:21:22,848
And now, number fifteen.

705
00:21:23,028 --> 00:21:24,599
The one I warned you about at

706
00:21:24,599 --> 00:21:25,141
the start.

707
00:21:25,491 --> 00:21:27,139
Change number fifteen.

708
00:21:27,319 --> 00:21:29,607
Twenty thousand dollars makes you feel richer

709
00:21:29,607 --> 00:21:30,268
than you are.

710
00:21:30,448 --> 00:21:32,178
And that feeling is the trap.

711
00:21:32,528 --> 00:21:34,385
It's called lifestyle creep.

712
00:21:34,565 --> 00:21:36,103
The balance looks big,

713
00:21:36,103 --> 00:21:37,640
so the car gets nicer,

714
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:39,457
the apartment gets bigger,

715
00:21:39,457 --> 00:21:41,973
and the monthly costs quietly go up.

716
00:21:42,153 --> 00:21:44,173
Then one day the cushion is gone,

717
00:21:44,173 --> 00:21:46,499
but the higher monthly bills are still

718
00:21:46,499 --> 00:21:46,866
there.

719
00:21:47,046 --> 00:21:48,925
You end up with a more expensive

720
00:21:48,925 --> 00:21:51,098
life and less protection than before.

721
00:21:51,448 --> 00:21:52,922
The fix is simple.

722
00:21:53,102 --> 00:21:55,470
Decide in advance what the cushion is

723
00:21:55,470 --> 00:21:55,726
for.

724
00:21:55,906 --> 00:21:57,190
It's for emergencies,

725
00:21:57,190 --> 00:21:59,331
for freedom, and for opportunities.

726
00:21:59,511 --> 00:22:00,927
It's not a shopping budget.

727
00:22:01,107 --> 00:22:03,157
When it's spent on an emergency,

728
00:22:03,157 --> 00:22:05,719
refilling it becomes the first priority,

729
00:22:05,719 --> 00:22:06,936
before any upgrade.

730
00:22:07,286 --> 00:22:10,806
Lifestyle creep is sneaky because every single

731
00:22:10,806 --> 00:22:12,719
upgrade feels reasonable.

732
00:22:12,899 --> 00:22:14,525
You deserve a nicer car.

733
00:22:14,705 --> 00:22:15,680
You work hard.

734
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:17,369
And maybe you do deserve it.

735
00:22:17,549 --> 00:22:19,151
The question is timing.

736
00:22:19,331 --> 00:22:20,544
A rule that works:

737
00:22:20,544 --> 00:22:22,229
when your income goes up,

738
00:22:22,229 --> 00:22:24,319
send at least half of the raise

739
00:22:24,319 --> 00:22:25,936
to savings or investing,

740
00:22:25,936 --> 00:22:27,621
and enjoy the other half.

741
00:22:27,801 --> 00:22:29,386
You still get a better life,

742
00:22:29,386 --> 00:22:31,480
but your cushion and your future grow

743
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:32,782
faster than your bills.

744
00:22:32,962 --> 00:22:34,931
Try this: next time you get a

745
00:22:34,931 --> 00:22:37,580
raise, set up the extra transfer before

746
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:40,229
the first bigger paycheck even arrives.

747
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:41,636
Quick pause.

748
00:22:41,816 --> 00:22:44,101
If these changes are already making you

749
00:22:44,101 --> 00:22:45,683
think about your own money,

750
00:22:45,683 --> 00:22:47,441
do me a favor and subscribe to

751
00:22:47,441 --> 00:22:48,144
the channel.

752
00:22:48,324 --> 00:22:50,255
It's free, it takes one second,

753
00:22:50,255 --> 00:22:52,311
and it helps me keep making these

754
00:22:52,311 --> 00:22:52,747
videos.

755
00:22:53,097 --> 00:22:55,368
Numbers are easier to feel when there's

756
00:22:55,368 --> 00:22:56,591
a person behind them.

757
00:22:56,771 --> 00:22:58,362
So let me walk you through three

758
00:22:58,362 --> 00:22:58,710
people.

759
00:22:58,890 --> 00:22:59,867
They're made up,

760
00:22:59,867 --> 00:23:01,698
but every one of them is built

761
00:23:01,698 --> 00:23:04,261
from situations that happen to real people

762
00:23:04,261 --> 00:23:05,299
every single day.

763
00:23:05,479 --> 00:23:07,779
Watch what twenty thousand dollars does in

764
00:23:07,779 --> 00:23:08,381
each story.

765
00:23:08,732 --> 00:23:09,370
Meet Maya.

766
00:23:09,550 --> 00:23:10,661
She's thirty one,

767
00:23:10,661 --> 00:23:11,903
works in marketing,

768
00:23:11,903 --> 00:23:14,517
and spends about four thousand dollars a

769
00:23:14,517 --> 00:23:15,432
month on rent,

770
00:23:15,432 --> 00:23:17,654
food, her car and everything else.

771
00:23:17,834 --> 00:23:19,208
On a Tuesday morning,

772
00:23:19,208 --> 00:23:21,171
her company announces layoffs,

773
00:23:21,171 --> 00:23:22,873
and her whole team is cut.

774
00:23:23,053 --> 00:23:23,796
No warning.

775
00:23:23,976 --> 00:23:25,318
Two weeks of severance,

776
00:23:25,318 --> 00:23:26,135
and that's it.

777
00:23:26,485 --> 00:23:28,952
Here's the difference twenty thousand dollars makes

778
00:23:28,952 --> 00:23:29,387
for Maya.

779
00:23:29,567 --> 00:23:31,982
With four thousand a month in costs,

780
00:23:31,982 --> 00:23:34,397
her savings cover about five months.

781
00:23:34,577 --> 00:23:36,639
That's five months to find the right

782
00:23:36,639 --> 00:23:37,956
job, not the first job.

783
00:23:38,136 --> 00:23:40,026
People who can wait for a better

784
00:23:40,026 --> 00:23:41,976
offer often come back at the same

785
00:23:41,976 --> 00:23:42,803
pay or higher.

786
00:23:42,983 --> 00:23:45,396
People who panic tend to accept less,

787
00:23:45,396 --> 00:23:47,874
and that lower salary follows them for

788
00:23:47,874 --> 00:23:48,265
years.

789
00:23:48,615 --> 00:23:50,507
Maya doesn't touch a credit card.

790
00:23:50,687 --> 00:23:53,013
She files for unemployment benefits,

791
00:23:53,013 --> 00:23:54,628
cuts a few subscriptions,

792
00:23:54,628 --> 00:23:57,212
and spends her mornings applying and her

793
00:23:57,212 --> 00:23:59,279
afternoons learning a new skill.

794
00:23:59,459 --> 00:24:01,895
Eleven weeks later she accepts a job

795
00:24:01,895 --> 00:24:04,060
that pays a little more than the

796
00:24:04,060 --> 00:24:04,602
old one.

797
00:24:04,782 --> 00:24:06,221
The layoff still hurt.

798
00:24:06,401 --> 00:24:08,123
But it was a bad season,

799
00:24:08,123 --> 00:24:09,200
not a disaster.

800
00:24:09,550 --> 00:24:10,502
Now meet Daniel.

801
00:24:10,682 --> 00:24:12,765
He's forty, a contractor,

802
00:24:12,765 --> 00:24:13,515
two kids.

803
00:24:13,695 --> 00:24:15,526
His truck's transmission fails,

804
00:24:15,526 --> 00:24:17,771
and the repair quote is three thousand

805
00:24:17,771 --> 00:24:19,070
eight hundred dollars.

806
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:20,182
The same week,

807
00:24:20,182 --> 00:24:22,646
a client offers him a bigger project,

808
00:24:22,646 --> 00:24:24,643
but he has to buy materials up

809
00:24:24,643 --> 00:24:26,973
front, about five thousand dollars,

810
00:24:26,973 --> 00:24:29,037
and he gets paid in sixty days.

811
00:24:29,387 --> 00:24:32,556
Without savings, Daniel has a terrible choice.

812
00:24:32,736 --> 00:24:34,294
He can put the repair on a

813
00:24:34,294 --> 00:24:36,690
card at over twenty percent interest and

814
00:24:36,690 --> 00:24:38,007
turn down the project.

815
00:24:38,187 --> 00:24:39,960
Or he can take a high cost

816
00:24:39,960 --> 00:24:42,142
loan and hope the client pays on

817
00:24:42,142 --> 00:24:42,483
time.

818
00:24:42,663 --> 00:24:44,604
With twenty thousand dollars,

819
00:24:44,604 --> 00:24:45,876
he fixes the truck,

820
00:24:45,876 --> 00:24:47,147
buys the materials,

821
00:24:47,147 --> 00:24:48,352
and takes the job.

822
00:24:48,532 --> 00:24:50,679
The project pays him far more than

823
00:24:50,679 --> 00:24:51,690
the repair cost.

824
00:24:51,870 --> 00:24:54,215
Savings didn't just protect Daniel.

825
00:24:54,395 --> 00:24:55,952
They let him say yes to an

826
00:24:55,952 --> 00:24:56,671
opportunity.

827
00:24:57,021 --> 00:24:59,517
And finally, meet Priya and Sam.

828
00:24:59,697 --> 00:25:01,602
Married, mid thirties,

829
00:25:01,602 --> 00:25:02,727
both working.

830
00:25:02,907 --> 00:25:05,545
For years, they argued about money every

831
00:25:05,545 --> 00:25:06,204
few weeks.

832
00:25:06,384 --> 00:25:08,068
Not about big things.

833
00:25:08,248 --> 00:25:09,656
About the dentist bill,

834
00:25:09,656 --> 00:25:11,247
the car insurance renewal,

835
00:25:11,247 --> 00:25:13,451
the friend's wedding they had to fly

836
00:25:13,451 --> 00:25:13,635
to.

837
00:25:13,815 --> 00:25:16,544
Every surprise expense turned into a fight

838
00:25:16,544 --> 00:25:18,169
about whose fault it was.

839
00:25:18,519 --> 00:25:20,394
They didn't fix it with a budgeting

840
00:25:20,394 --> 00:25:20,609
app.

841
00:25:20,789 --> 00:25:22,855
They fixed it by building a shared

842
00:25:22,855 --> 00:25:25,105
cushion, slowly, over about two and a

843
00:25:25,105 --> 00:25:27,779
half years, until it reached twenty thousand

844
00:25:27,779 --> 00:25:28,265
dollars.

845
00:25:28,445 --> 00:25:30,582
The surprise bills still came.

846
00:25:30,762 --> 00:25:32,714
But now a dentist bill was just

847
00:25:32,714 --> 00:25:34,477
a transfer, not an argument.

848
00:25:34,657 --> 00:25:36,178
Sam put it simply.

849
00:25:36,358 --> 00:25:37,716
We still talk about money.

850
00:25:37,896 --> 00:25:39,614
We just don't fight about it anymore.

851
00:25:39,965 --> 00:25:41,242
Three different lives.

852
00:25:41,422 --> 00:25:43,192
A layoff, a broken truck,

853
00:25:43,192 --> 00:25:45,033
a marriage under pressure.

854
00:25:45,213 --> 00:25:46,435
And in every story,

855
00:25:46,435 --> 00:25:48,301
the money did the same thing.

856
00:25:48,481 --> 00:25:49,467
It bought time,

857
00:25:49,467 --> 00:25:50,650
it bought choices,

858
00:25:50,650 --> 00:25:51,964
and it bought peace.

859
00:25:52,144 --> 00:25:54,201
That's the pattern you'll see again and

860
00:25:54,201 --> 00:25:56,468
again, and it's why twenty thousand dollars

861
00:25:56,468 --> 00:25:58,367
matters more than it looks on paper.

862
00:25:58,717 --> 00:26:01,496
So, what really changes at twenty thousand

863
00:26:01,496 --> 00:26:02,026
